{"id":43,"course_id":14,"title":"Cluster Upgrades and Add-on Compatibility","summary":"Plan upgrades as a compatibility project, not a button click.","sequence":4,"body_simplified":"## Upgrade program\nProduction EKS upgrades touch Kubernetes API versions, managed control plane version, node versions, add-ons, controllers, CRDs, and client tooling. Advanced teams rehearse upgrades.\n- Inventory deprecated APIs before upgrading.\n- Upgrade add-ons in a supported order.\n- Roll node groups or Karpenter capacity carefully.\n- Test admission policies, controllers, and Helm charts against the target version.\n- Keep rollback and pause points clear.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl version\n$ kubectl api-resources\n$ kubectl get crd\n$ helm list -A\n$ kubectl get pods -A | grep -E 'CrashLoopBackOff|ImagePullBackOff'\nUse version checks and manifests locally where possible; do not upgrade real clusters from this repo.\n\n## Tradeoff\nWaiting too long increases version skew and deprecated API risk. Upgrading too fast without rehearsals risks controller and workload outages.","body_traditional":"## Upgrade program\nProduction EKS upgrades touch Kubernetes API versions, managed control plane version, node versions, add-ons, controllers, CRDs, and client tooling. Advanced teams rehearse upgrades.\n- Inventory deprecated APIs before upgrading.\n- Upgrade add-ons in a supported order.\n- Roll node groups or Karpenter capacity carefully.\n- Test admission policies, controllers, and Helm charts against the target version.\n- Keep rollback and pause points clear.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl version\n$ kubectl api-resources\n$ kubectl get crd\n$ helm list -A\n$ kubectl get pods -A | grep -E 'CrashLoopBackOff|ImagePullBackOff'\nUse version checks and manifests locally where possible; do not upgrade real clusters from this repo.\n\n## Tradeoff\nWaiting too long increases version skew and deprecated API risk. Upgrading too fast without rehearsals risks controller and workload outages.","pinyin":"Scenario: Ingress resources use an API removed in the next cluster version.\nChecklist:\n- Search manifests and Helm renders for deprecated apiVersions.\n- Upgrade charts or manifests before the control plane upgrade.\n- Validate generated YAML against the target version.\n- Track owners for every incompatible resource.\nCommands:\n$ grep -R \"extensions/v1beta1\\|networking.k8s.io/v1beta1\" manifests/\n$ helm template checkout charts/checkout -f values/prod.yaml > rendered.yaml","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-production-eks-architecture","course_category":"EKS","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":186,"simplified":"Version skew","traditional":"Version skew","pinyin":"Compatibility","definition":"Differences between Kubernetes component or client versions."},{"id":187,"simplified":"Deprecated API","traditional":"Deprecated API","pinyin":"Upgrade risk","definition":"An API version scheduled for removal in a later Kubernetes version."},{"id":188,"simplified":"Control plane","traditional":"Control plane","pinyin":"Cluster core","definition":"The Kubernetes API and controllers managed by EKS."},{"id":189,"simplified":"Node rollout","traditional":"Node rollout","pinyin":"Capacity upgrade","definition":"Replacing worker nodes with versions or images compatible with the new cluster."},{"id":190,"simplified":"Pause point","traditional":"Pause point","pinyin":"Upgrade control","definition":"A planned checkpoint where an upgrade can stop safely for validation."}],"flashcards":[{"id":112,"lesson_id":43,"prompt":"Why inventory APIs before upgrade?","answer":"Removed APIs can break applies, controllers, or workloads after the upgrade.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":113,"lesson_id":43,"prompt":"What components besides the control plane need upgrade planning?","answer":"Nodes, add-ons, controllers, CRDs, Helm charts, and clients.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":114,"lesson_id":43,"prompt":"What is the risk of waiting too long?","answer":"Version skew grows and deprecated API removals become harder to handle.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
